  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nA mid/upper ridge axis extended from the Lower Mississippi Valley \narea NNW across the Central Plains while a stationary front was \nbisecting Kansas early this afternoon. Low clouds and fog were \nobserved north of the front impacting much of northern and western \nKansas. This frontal boundary is progged to meander over the area \nthrough tonight before mixing northward as we move into the day on \nFriday. This is expected to result in another round of low ceilings \nand areas of fog developing along and north of the boundary again \ntonight. We may also see a sharp temperature gradient, especially \nwhere low clouds persist. Some elevated showers and thunderstorms \nwill be possible across parts of central and northeast Kansas this \nevening and tonight, aided by a developing LLJ. PWATs are progged to \nrise to 1.25+ inches supporting efficient rainfall. We could see a \nstrong storm or two with gusty winds and hail given elevated CAPE \n~1500 J/KG this evening. The initial development could be over \ncentral KS before the activity focuses further north across \nnortheast KS where we will see a higher potential for some pockets \nof excessive rain.\n\nSome storms  may be ongoing across parts of northeast KS early on \nFriday before we see a brief lull during the late morning/early \nafternoon hours. Increasing probabilities for rain are expected to \nreturn as we move into the afternoon and evening hours as a \nnegatively tilted shortwave trough lifts northeast towards the area. \nWith steep mid-lvl lapse rates initially progged, we could see some \nembedded thunderstorms at times, especially across northern KS later \nin the day and perhaps lingering into the evening hours. Some drier \nair could be wrapped into parts of southeast KS, limiting rainfall \npotential as we move into the evening and overnight hours as the \nsystem quickly lifts north of the area. Some low pops were \nmaintained on Sat but lingering clouds should keep temperatures near \nclimate normals with highs mostly in the 60s.\n\nShortwave ridging will return to the area on Sunday with mostly dry \nweather conditions anticipated during the day. With some breaks in \nthe clouds, afternoon highs may rise into the lower 70s in some \nareas while parts of central and western KS will see more seasonable \nhighs in the 60s. Another robust mid/upper trough is progged to \nbuild over the Rockies late Sunday/Sunday night. Showers and \nthunderstorms are expected to become more numerous Sunday \nnight/early Monday morning as the exit region of the upper jet \nbuilds over the Southern Plains. The pattern may support a few \nstrong/severe storms as we move through the day on Monday but given \nthe potential for more widespread activity and potential training, \npockets of heavy rain may be a bigger concern. High pops were \nmaintained Mon night into Tuesday as numerous showers are \nanticipated under the upper low. A cold front will usher in more \nseasonable air as we move into Wed-Thu with highs in the low to mid \n60s. Some low pops were maintained on Wed due to uncertainty in the \ntiming of the mid/upper trough. Dry weather conditions are expected \nto return by late Wed into Thu.",
